1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,013
Hej. This is Liam, in for Bear. Claude's desk buddy isn't a product feature. It's an

2
00:00:05,033 --> 00:00:09,103
opt-in API for makers, so the real question is how you build one.

3
00:00:09,173 --> 00:00:13,775
Three terms. B L E, Bluetooth Low Energy: a low-power wireless link. A permission

4
00:00:13,795 --> 00:00:18,396
prompt: Claude asking before it runs a tool. And a reference repo: one worked

5
00:00:18,416 --> 00:00:20,047
example, made to be copied.

6
00:00:20,117 --> 00:00:25,133
Here's the desk. A laptop runs the Claude app, on Mac or Windows. Beside it sits a

7
00:00:25,153 --> 00:00:29,577
tiny device with a screen and buttons. The app can connect your Cowork and Claude

8
00:00:29,597 --> 00:00:31,354
Code sessions to it, over Bluetooth.

9
00:00:31,424 --> 00:00:36,594
That bridge is off by default, and you opt in. Turn on developer mode, open Hardware

10
00:00:36,614 --> 00:00:40,812
Buddy, click connect, and pick your device. After that, it reconnects by itself.

11
00:00:40,832 --> 00:00:43,407
It's for makers, not an official product feature.

12
00:00:43,477 --> 00:00:47,924
Now the app sends a snapshot whenever something changes, and every ten seconds

13
00:00:47,944 --> 00:00:52,391
anyway: how many sessions are running or waiting, plus a few recent transcript

14
00:00:52,411 --> 00:00:55,140
lines, newest first. The tiny screen scrolls them.

15
00:00:55,210 --> 00:01:00,519
The team's own example is a desk pet, on an E S P thirty-two board. It sleeps when

16
00:01:00,539 --> 00:01:03,183
nothing's happening. Start a session, and it wakes up.

17
00:01:03,253 --> 00:01:07,492
Now Claude wants to run a Bash command, and it needs your permission. The snapshot

18
00:01:07,512 --> 00:01:12,603
carries a prompt: the tool, a short hint, and an ID. It shows up on the device too,

19
00:01:12,623 --> 00:01:14,874
and the pet gets impatient. Its light blinks.

20
00:01:14,944 --> 00:01:19,775
Press the front button, and the device sends back one line: that same ID, and the

21
00:01:19,795 --> 00:01:23,717
decision, once. Once approves that one tool call. The other button sends deny.

22
00:01:23,737 --> 00:01:25,839
Approve quickly, and the pet shows hearts.

23
00:01:25,909 --> 00:01:30,074
That link carries transcript snippets and tool hints, and an unencrypted one can be

24
00:01:30,094 --> 00:01:34,857
read by anyone in radio range with a cheap dongle. So the reference says to require

25
00:01:34,877 --> 00:01:39,341
encrypted pairing. The device shows a six-digit pass key, you type it into the app,

26
00:01:39,361 --> 00:01:41,732
and the link is encrypted from then on.

27
00:01:41,802 --> 00:01:46,612
And you don't need any of this code. REFERENCE dot md is the contract: advertise the

28
00:01:46,632 --> 00:01:51,139
Nordic UART Service, a standard way to send text over Bluetooth, then trade one line

29
00:01:51,159 --> 00:01:56,271
of JSON at a time. An Arduino, or a Raspberry Pi with a Bluetooth dongle, will do.

30
00:01:56,291 --> 00:01:59,289
The repo's own advice: the best contribution is a fork.

31
00:01:59,359 --> 00:02:03,248
Your turn. Paste this into Claude: Here is REFERENCE dot md from Anthropic's claude

32
00:02:03,268 --> 00:02:07,995
desktop buddy repo: paste it. Help me plan a tiny device of my own that speaks this

33
00:02:08,015 --> 00:02:11,625
protocol: name your device. List what its screen shows from each snapshot, which

34
00:02:11,645 --> 00:02:15,814
permission prompts it may approve with a button press, and what it must never do

35
00:02:15,834 --> 00:02:19,723
without a person. Plan only; no firmware yet. Then check two things yourself. Find

36
00:02:19,743 --> 00:02:24,191
every approval in the plan. Is each one a button press? And open REFERENCE dot md.

37
00:02:24,211 --> 00:02:26,425
Do the plan's field names match it exactly?

38
00:02:26,495 --> 00:02:30,284
Claude on Your Desk. At Nik Bear Brown.
